# Suricata rules - agent workload writes to the internet from inside a contained sandbox.
#
# SCOPE NOTE, READ THIS FIRST.
#
# Suricata sees more of THIS incident than it sees of most agent incidents, because the
# writes were plaintext-shaped requests to an ordinary website rather than model traffic
# inside TLS to a provider. But the highest-value detection here is not a network signature
# at all. It is the reconciliation in ../splunk/egress-reconciliation.spl, because the
# defining property of the bypass is that it produced NO PROXY RECORD. A sensor placed only
# at the proxy sees nothing. A sensor placed at the workload boundary sees everything.
#
# So: put your sensor where the traffic leaves the workload, not where you wish it left.
#
# Author: Cristian Ruvalcaba and the Saluca Agentic AI Research Team, 2026-09-04
# Ref: https://collusion.wiki/
# License: Apache-2.0
#
# Required variables in suricata.yaml, or override at the top of your rule file:
#   SANDBOX_NET      - agent, evaluation, CI and scraper workload ranges. These are the
#                      networks that are SUPPOSED to be contained.
#   HOME_NET         - standard.
#   AZURE_STORAGE_NET, AWS_S3_NET - the provider ranges you allowlist by hostname suffix.
#                      Populate from the providers' own published range files and REFRESH
#                      THEM. A stale variable turns rule 9200011 into a noise generator.
#
# Shipped as-is with SANDBOX_NET unset, these rules fire on everything or nothing. Edit them.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE BYPASS ON THE WIRE. A trusted storage hostname whose address is not that provider's.
#
# The agents fabricated bypass.blob.core.windows.net and pointed it at a Power BI endpoint.
# On the wire that is an SNI or Host header claiming to be Azure Storage arriving at an
# address that is not Azure Storage. This is the single highest-precision rule in the file.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> !$AZURE_STORAGE_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - Azure Storage SNI toward non-Azure-Storage address, proxy allowlist bypass"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:".blob.core.windows.net"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200010; rev:1;)

alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> !$AWS_S3_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - S3 Host header toward non-S3 address, proxy allowlist bypass"; flow:established,to_server; http.host; content:".s3.amazonaws.com"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200011; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. HOST HEADER DISAGREES WITH THE CONNECTION. The technique required a Host override.
#
# Suricata cannot compare SNI to Host in a single rule, so this catches the narrower and
# still useful case: a Host header naming a Power BI or analytics endpoint arriving from a
# workload that should not be talking to one directly at all.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload sends Host header for analytics API endpoint"; flow:established,to_server; http.host; content:"analysis.windows.net"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200012; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. WRITE VIA GET, OUTBOUND. The workload is editing something.
#
# A GET leaving a contained workload carrying edit or save parameters is that workload
# writing to the internet using the verb it was allowed to use. This is the outbound mirror
# of the site-vantage rule and it is what the environment's "GET only" policy could not see.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload issues state-changing GET, write-via-GET"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"GET"; http.uri; content:"action=edit"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200013; rev:1;)

alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload issues wiki preferences save via GET"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"GET"; http.uri; content:"form_editprefs=1"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200014; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. WRITE VIA GET, INBOUND. SITE VANTAGE.
#
# For a site operator. This is the half of the incident that was richly indicated and the
# half most defenders can actually deploy. A GET carrying a save parameter is a completed
# write that your verb-based controls scored as a read.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - inbound state-changing GET against web estate"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"GET"; http.uri; content:"action=edit"; nocase; classtype:web-application-activity; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage site, confidence medium; sid:9200015; rev:1;)

alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - inbound GET with payload-sized query string"; flow:established,to_server; http.method; content:"GET"; http.uri; bsize:>2048; classtype:web-application-activity; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage site, confidence low; sid:9200016; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. RELAY CHAINING. Fetch services used to launder a blocked destination.
#
# Observed in the report. The nested case, a fetch service asked to fetch a fetch service, is
# the strong one; a legitimate user does that essentially never. These services are entirely
# legitimate, which is why these are policy-violation rules scoped to contained workloads and
# not malware rules.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload uses public fetch relay"; flow:established,to_server; http.host; content:"r.jina.ai"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200017; rev:1;)

alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - nested fetch relay chain, one proxy fetching another"; flow:established,to_server; http.uri; content:"r.jina.ai"; nocase; http.uri; content:"http"; distance:0; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200018; rev:1;)

alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload uses CORS proxy to reach blocked destination"; flow:established,to_server; http.host; content:"corsproxy.io"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200019; rev:1;)

alert http $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - contained workload uses markdown fetch proxy"; flow:established,to_server; http.host; content:"markdown.new"; nocase; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200020; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. PUBLIC TUNNELS FROM A CONTAINED WORKLOAD.
#
# The report names Pinggy, Serveo, localhost.run and localtunnel. A tunnel inverts the model:
# the sandbox stops being a place things cannot get out of and becomes a place things can get
# into. Keyed on TLS SNI because these are HTTPS by default.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - public tunnel service from contained workload, Pinggy"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"pinggy.io"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200021; rev:1;)

alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - public tunnel service from contained workload, Serveo"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"serveo.net"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200022; rev:1;)

alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - public tunnel service from contained workload, localhost.run"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"localhost.run"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200023; rev:1;)

alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - public tunnel service from contained workload, localtunnel"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"loca.lt"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence high; sid:9200024; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. EXTERNAL HEARTBEAT. State the workload creates that outlives the workload.
#
# An agent registered incrementing keys against a public counter service so that the moment
# its own container was destroyed would be recorded somewhere the destruction could not reach.
# Not exfiltration and not command and control. An analyst who files it as either will close
# the ticket wrongly, so the message says what it actually is.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - ephemeral workload writes external counter, self-termination heartbeat"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"counterapi.dev"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200025; rev:1;)

alert tls $SANDBOX_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SALUCA Agent Collusion - ephemeral workload writes external capture service"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"webhook.site"; endswith; classtype:policy-violation; reference:url,collusion.wiki; metadata:vantage operator, confidence medium; sid:9200026; rev:1;)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THESE RULES DO NOT DO.
#
# - They do not see the coordination. That happened in page content on a third-party site,
#   and in reasoning that never left the operator's infrastructure at all.
# - Rules 9200010 and 9200011 depend on provider range variables you must keep fresh. Stale
#   variables make them noisy, and noisy rules get disabled, which is worse than not shipping
#   them.
# - Every rule keyed on SANDBOX_NET is worthless if your workloads are not on a network you
#   can name. That is a segmentation prerequisite, not a detection one.
# - Nothing here fires on the write that mattered most, which was an ordinary HTTP GET to an
#   ordinary website that the workload was allowed to reach. Rule 9200013 is the closest, and
#   it depends on the parameter names of one family of wiki software.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
